// A stack overflow is a NAMED failure, like every other one. Roadmap B7.
//
// `DESIGN.md`: nothing in the language should fail anonymously. This was the one exception — a
// recursion with no base case died of a raw SIGSEGV, exit 139, with nothing on stderr. Every other
// way a Burxt program can fail says what happened and exits 70.
//
// Worth knowing how it was verified, because the obvious check is wrong: at `-O2` LLVM turns some
// recursions into loops, so `1 + deep(n - 1)` on a big `n` simply runs and the fault never happens.
// A test written only against a default build would have passed on a compiler that had no guard at
// all. It was measured at `-O0` as well, where the recursion stays a recursion — which is a use for
// C1's flag that has nothing to do with debugging.
//
// The guard is a floor: `main` asks `getrlimit` how much stack this process was given and records
// the address where it runs out, less a margin so the message itself has room to print. Every
// function compares its own frame against that floor. `getrlimit` rather than a constant because
// the stack is whatever the OS gave — 8 MB by default, unlimited under one `ulimit`, small inside
// a container — and a hardcoded guess would be wrong in both directions.
//
// The check is in EVERY function rather than only in ones a call graph shows to be recursive. That
// is deliberate: a static call graph cannot see recursion through a `dynamic` call, and a guard
// with a hole in it is worse than none, because the hole is where someone writes the interesting
// recursion. Measured cost: about 7% of object size.
//
// `return down(n - 1)` in tail position would NOT do this — a tail call reuses the frame and the
// stack does not grow. The `1 +` is what forces a frame to stay alive, and it is why the message
// says so.
function down(n: Int) -> Int {
    if n <= 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1 + down(n - 1);
}

print(down(100000000));
